# Stream apply order for this project, most-dependent-first.
#
# ACROSS streams order normally does NOT matter -- streams touch disjoint files
# and commute (n64/CLAUDE.md). SuperMario64 is the exception, twice over:
#
# `upstreamable-standard-c` FIRST (2026-09-22). It rewrites the decomp's
# assembly-isms into standard C and is bound for upstream, so it must be the BASE
# the personal streams sit on: the personal patches then apply to the code as upstream would
# see it after merging, and a conflict surfaces here, on our side, rather than
# in a rebase we cannot do. It touches many src/game, src/engine, src/goddard
# and src/audio files -- including mario.c, mario_actions_airborne.c and the
# book's marker files -- so it overlaps both streams below. When it changes,
# replay: ./fetch.sh && ./apply.sh, then re-export whichever personal patch
# stopped applying (tasks/mario64-assembly-isms-to-standard-c.md, Phase C).
#
# `cheats` before `book`: both touch src/game/mario.c. They do not collide
# today (the hunks sit ~370 lines apart), but the book grows a doc-region per
# chapter and covers Mario's action machine, which is exactly what the cheats
# edit -- so the two will meet. The cheats are the substantive code, the
# markers are commentary on whatever the code ends up being.
#
# The `upstreamable-*` PR units `upstreamable-ci-appimage` and
# `upstreamable-fedora-shaderc-deps` are not listed -- they touch only docker/,
# the CI workflow, and docs/building.md, so they are disjoint from everything and
# apply afterwards, alphabetically. (`upstreamable-standard-c` IS listed, above,
# because the personal streams depend on it.)
#
# One stream per line; listed streams apply first, in this order. Comments and
# blank lines are ignored.
upstreamable-standard-c
cheats
book
